title: Module-level engine enums are invisible to the LSP (47 enums, 52 unresolvable value names)
updated_at: "2026-08-29T17:30:49Z"
---
Found by the B044 lane's game probe, 2026-08-30. Pre-existing; independent of
B044, which merely stopped masking 3 of its symptoms.

## Defect
The Nadeo dump's `ns` map is `namespace -> type name -> type`, so it can only
carry an enum nested inside a CLASS. Every enum declared at MODULE level is
therefore never indexed.

Counted against `~/OpenplanetNext/Openplanet.h`: **47 module-level enums**,
carrying **306 distinct value names**. Of those:

- **251** also appear in a class-nested or Core enum, so a bare reference
  resolves anyway — invisible luck, not correctness.
- **52** resolve NOWHERE. These are pre-existing false positives: a plugin
  referencing one gets `No matching symbol` from the LSP while the game
  compiles it fine. Never noticed because nothing exercised them.
- **3** (`DoubleJump`, `Jump`, `TechJump`, all values of
  `Scene::ESceneCharVisNotice`) were resolving only BY ACCIDENT, through the
  permissive anonymous-enum scan that B044 narrowed. B044 turned these into
  visible false positives; they are 3/55 of this defect, not a B044 regression.

## Evidence
Established two ways that agree exactly:
1. A live RemoteBuild probe (Trackmania + Openplanet 1.29.5, 2026-08-30)
   against 261 candidate names returned 258 `No matching symbol` errors — the
   3 accepted are exactly the `ESceneCharVisNotice` values above.
2. Static analysis of the dump's `ns` structure, independent of the probe,
   predicted the same 3.

Registered in `todo-false-positives.txt` by the B044 lane with a fix
direction.

## Why this is worth fixing
It is the same class of defect as B016 (engine enums silently dropped from the
index), one level up: B016 recovered enums the dump named ambiguously, this is
enums the dump's SHAPE cannot express at all. The consequence is the more
damaging direction — false POSITIVES, where the LSP rejects code the game
accepts. 52 names is a small surface, but each one is a hard error on valid
code, and the user has no way to tell it is the tool that is wrong.

## Work
- Teach the Nadeo loader to index module-level enums. The `ns` map cannot
  carry them, so the source is elsewhere — determine where the dump exposes
  module-level declarations (check whether `Openplanet.h` is itself parseable
  as the authority, or whether another section of the JSON carries them)
  before designing the key scheme.
- Key them so they do not collide with class-nested enums of the same name.
- Decide whether their values participate in the bare-name scan. They SHOULD:
  unlike B044's anonymous enums, a module-level enum HAS a name the game
  accepts, which is exactly why the game resolves `DoubleJump`.
- Re-check the 3 known-FP names in
  `tests/fixtures/issue-repros/b044-anon-enum-value-names/` — they are marked
  `KNOWN FP` inline and must flip to resolving when this lands.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)
Red: a fixture referencing `DoubleJump` / `Jump` / `TechJump` reports
`No matching symbol` today and is clean after. Same for a sample of the 52
that resolve nowhere — pick at least 5 across different modules.

Variants (per CLAUDE.md, generalize):
- a module-level enum value that ALSO exists in a class-nested enum -> still
  resolves, and to the right one (guard against the 251 masking a bad fix)
- a module-level enum name used qualified (`Scene::ESceneCharVisNotice`) ->
  resolves as a type
- a value used qualified through its enum -> resolves
- collision between a module-level enum name and a class-nested type name in
  the same namespace -> deterministic, documented resolution
- the B044 narrowing still holds: anonymous inline member enums stay
  unspellable (this fix must not reopen the 258)
- determinism: two loads of identical bytes yield identical indexes
- `typedb diff` of the fixture against itself stays empty

## Gate
FP fixture ERROR counts must not RISE. A drop is the expected win if any
fixture references one of the 52. Measure two binaries on the same machine at
the same time against `tests/fixtures/` — B019 made output depend on the local
plugin install, and the live plugin checkouts are NOT the repo fixtures
(current repo baselines: 14/1485, 5/276, 2/35, 4/2, 4/4).

